# Pizza Bot — example environment. Copy to `.env` and fill in as needed.
# The api-server backfills unset vars from `.env.local` then `.env`, searched in
# the data root, the current working directory, then the repo root (Node's
# process.loadEnvFile; a missing file is skipped). An already-exported shell var
# ALWAYS wins over a file value, so shell exports / SSO / a process manager still
# take precedence. Loading `.env.local` first means it overrides `.env` in the
# same directory. Other entrypoints (the CLI) read only the process environment
# — export there. Values shown are defaults.

# ── Model provider — Bedrock is the reference (any real run needs one provider) ─
# Bedrock uses the standard AWS credential chain (env vars, SSO, or a named
# profile) plus a region with model access. Anthropic, Google Gemini, OpenAI,
# OpenRouter, and Ollama are also supported; add their keys from Settings > Providers
# (stored in the OS keychain, not here). No provider configured → runs fail at
# the first model call.
AWS_REGION=us-west-2
# AWS_PROFILE=my-sso-profile
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=...
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=...
# AWS_SESSION_TOKEN=...

# ── Model selection ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The default agent model, as `<provider>:<model-id>` (e.g. `anthropic:...`).
# PIZZA_MODEL=bedrock:global.anthropic.claude-sonnet-5
# Max output tokens per model call (default 8192, keeping headroom so adaptive
# thinking can't blank a turn).
# PIZZA_MAX_TOKENS=8192

# ── Server / transport ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The api-server binds to loopback by default. Remote binding requires all three
# settings below so it cannot accidentally expose privileged APIs.
# PORT=8080
# PIZZA_HOST=0.0.0.0
# PIZZA_API_TOKEN=replace-with-at-least-32-random-characters
# PIZZA_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://pizza.example
# Static web builds read their API base/token from the deploy-time
# dist/pizza-config.js file; see README.md. Do not put tokens in URLs.
# Serve a built browser app from this server instead, on the same origin. The
# generated /pizza-config.js then publishes PIZZA_API_TOKEN to every client that
# can reach the listener, so restrict the port rather than the app.
# PIZZA_WEB_DIR=/abs/path/to/apps/web/dist
# The CLI is a thin HTTP client (it boots no runtime); point it at a server:
# PIZZA_REMOTE_URL=http://localhost:8080

# ── Data + discovery directories ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where the durable stores live (checkpoints.sqlite / store.sqlite / app.sqlite).
# PIZZA_DATA_ROOT=$HOME/.pizza-bot-oss
# Override the scanned plugins/ directory (default: repo-relative).
# PIZZA_PLUGINS_DIR=/abs/path/to/plugins
# Additional independently distributed plugin roots (path-delimited).
# PIZZA_EXTERNAL_PLUGINS_DIRS=/abs/path/to/plugins

# ── MCP-server secrets ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Put values referenced as ${ENV_VAR} in a user `.mcp.json` or plugin manifest
# here. They are expanded only when the MCP connection starts.
# EXAMPLE_MCP_API_KEY=...

# ── Dev toggles ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Node binary used to launch stdio MCP servers, if auto-resolution needs help.
# PIZZA_MCP_NODE_PATH=/usr/local/bin/node

# ── Desktop app packaging / code-signing (build-time only) ──────────────────
# Only needed when building/notarizing the Electron app; see apps/desktop-shell.
# forge.config.ts reads these from the repo-root `.env` at packaging time. Each
# step is independently optional: absent credentials skip that step, while
# invalid configured credentials fail packaging. Check the `[forge] signing as:`
# and `[forge] notarizing as:` lines.
#
# Signing needs the full `Developer ID Application` name from
# `security find-identity -v -p codesigning`, either verbatim …
# APPLE_SIGNING_IDENTITY=Developer ID Application: Your Name (TEAM123456)
# … or assembled from these two (equivalent; do not quote the name):
# APPLE_DEVELOPER_NAME=Your Name
# APPLE_TEAM_ID=TEAM123456
#
# Notarization additionally needs an app-specific password (NOT your Apple ID
# password) from https://appleid.apple.com/account/manage, plus APPLE_TEAM_ID.
# APPLE_ID=you@example.com
# APPLE_ID_PASSWORD=xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx
#
# Windows Authenticode signing uses a local PFX path and its password. CI stores
# the base64-encoded PFX as WINDOWS_CERTIFICATE_BASE64 instead and writes this
# temporary file itself.
# WINDOWS_CERTIFICATE_FILE=C:\secure\pizza-bot-signing.pfx
# WINDOWS_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD=replace-with-pfx-password
